# Native battery monitoring: Pigeon commands and an event stream (Android + iOS)

## Check `battery_plus` first

The maintained [`battery_plus`](https://pub.dev/packages/battery_plus) plugin already provides most of this on Android and iOS:

- battery level
- a charging-state stream (`onBatteryStateChanged`)
- power-save mode (`isInBatterySaveMode`)

If it covers your commands, add it and wrap it in the Dart adapter shown in step 2. You don't need native code.

Write your own bridge only if you need something it lacks, for example:

- one combined snapshot stream
- power-save changes as events
- product-specific native behavior

The rest of this answer assumes that's the case.

**Assumed commands** (tell me yours if they differ):
- `getSnapshot()`: level, charging state and power-save mode
- `isPowerSaveModeEnabled()`
- `watchBattery()`: a stream of snapshots

**Boundary choice:** a Pigeon `@HostApi` for the commands and a Pigeon `@EventChannelApi` for the stream. Pigeon 22.7 or later supports event channels for Kotlin and Swift. This gives typed messages on all three sides. This setup is for in-app code. If it should become a reusable or published plugin, package shape and federation belong to package design, so that becomes a separate step.

---

## 1. Contract: `pigeons/battery.dart`

```dart
import 'package:pigeon/pigeon.dart';

@ConfigurePigeon(PigeonOptions(
  dartOut: 'lib/src/battery/battery_api.g.dart',
  kotlinOut: 'android/app/src/main/kotlin/com/example/app/battery/BatteryApi.g.kt',
  kotlinOptions: KotlinOptions(package: 'com.example.app.battery'),
  swiftOut: 'ios/Runner/Battery/BatteryApi.g.swift',
))
enum BatteryStateMessage { unknown, unplugged, charging, full }

class BatterySnapshotMessage {
  BatterySnapshotMessage({this.level, required this.state, required this.powerSaveMode});
  int? level; // 0..100; null when the platform can't report it (e.g. iOS simulator)
  BatteryStateMessage state;
  bool powerSaveMode;
}

@HostApi()
abstract class BatteryHostApi {
  BatterySnapshotMessage getSnapshot();
  bool isPowerSaveModeEnabled();
}

@EventChannelApi()
abstract class BatteryEventApi {
  BatterySnapshotMessage watchBattery();
}
```

Generate the code with `dart run pigeon --input pigeons/battery.dart`.

The `*Message` types exist only at the channel boundary. App code never sees them.

## 2. Dart adapter: domain types, validation and error mapping

```dart
// lib/src/battery/battery_monitor.dart
import 'package:flutter/foundation.dart';
import 'package:flutter/services.dart';
import 'battery_api.g.dart';

enum ChargingState { unknown, discharging, charging, full }

@immutable
class BatteryStatus {
  const BatteryStatus({required this.levelPercent, required this.state, required this.powerSaveMode});
  final int? levelPercent;
  final ChargingState state;
  final bool powerSaveMode;

  @override
  bool operator ==(Object o) =>
      o is BatteryStatus && o.levelPercent == levelPercent && o.state == state && o.powerSaveMode == powerSaveMode;
  @override
  int get hashCode => Object.hash(levelPercent, state, powerSaveMode);
}

class BatteryException implements Exception {
  const BatteryException(this.code, [this.message]);
  final String code;
  final String? message;
  @override
  String toString() => 'BatteryException($code): ${message ?? ''}';
}

abstract interface class BatteryMonitor {
  Future<BatteryStatus> current();
  Future<bool> isPowerSaveModeEnabled();
  Stream<BatteryStatus> watch();

  factory BatteryMonitor() {
    if (!kIsWeb &&
        (defaultTargetPlatform == TargetPlatform.android || defaultTargetPlatform == TargetPlatform.iOS)) {
      return PlatformBatteryMonitor();
    }
    return const UnsupportedBatteryMonitor();
  }
}

class PlatformBatteryMonitor implements BatteryMonitor {
  PlatformBatteryMonitor({BatteryHostApi? api, Stream<BatterySnapshotMessage> Function()? events})
      : _api = api ?? BatteryHostApi(),
        _events = events ?? watchBattery;

  final BatteryHostApi _api;
  final Stream<BatterySnapshotMessage> Function() _events;

  @override
  Future<BatteryStatus> current() => _guard(() async => _toDomain(await _api.getSnapshot()));

  @override
  Future<bool> isPowerSaveModeEnabled() => _guard(_api.isPowerSaveModeEnabled);

  @override
  Stream<BatteryStatus> watch() => _events()
      .map(_toDomain)
      .handleError((Object e) => throw _map(e), test: (e) => e is PlatformException)
      .distinct(); // Android's battery broadcast fires on voltage/temperature changes too

  static BatteryStatus _toDomain(BatterySnapshotMessage m) {
    final level = m.level;
    if (level != null && (level < 0 || level > 100)) {
      throw BatteryException('invalid_level', 'Native layer reported $level');
    }
    return BatteryStatus(
      levelPercent: level,
      state: switch (m.state) {
        BatteryStateMessage.unplugged => ChargingState.discharging,
        BatteryStateMessage.charging => ChargingState.charging,
        BatteryStateMessage.full => ChargingState.full,
        BatteryStateMessage.unknown => ChargingState.unknown,
      },
      powerSaveMode: m.powerSaveMode,
    );
  }

  static Future<T> _guard<T>(Future<T> Function() call) async {
    try {
      return await call();
    } on PlatformException catch (e) {
      throw _map(e);
    }
  }

  static BatteryException _map(Object e) => e is PlatformException
      ? BatteryException(e.code, e.message)
      : BatteryException('unknown', e.toString());
}

/// Explicit behavior instead of a MissingPluginException at runtime.
class UnsupportedBatteryMonitor implements BatteryMonitor {
  const UnsupportedBatteryMonitor();
  static const _e = BatteryException('unsupported_platform', 'Battery monitoring is Android/iOS only');
  @override
  Future<BatteryStatus> current() => Future.error(_e);
  @override
  Future<bool> isPowerSaveModeEnabled() => Future.error(_e);
  @override
  Stream<BatteryStatus> watch() => Stream.error(_e);
}
```

## 3. Android (Kotlin)

```kotlin
// android/app/src/main/kotlin/com/example/app/battery/BatteryBridge.kt
package com.example.app.battery

import android.content.*
import android.os.BatteryManager
import android.os.PowerManager
import androidx.core.content.ContextCompat

class BatteryBridge(private val context: Context) : BatteryHostApi {
    private val powerManager = context.getSystemService(PowerManager::class.java)

    override fun getSnapshot(): BatterySnapshotMessage = snapshot()
    override fun isPowerSaveModeEnabled(): Boolean = powerManager.isPowerSaveMode

    fun snapshot(): BatterySnapshotMessage {
        // Sticky broadcast: returns the last value without registering a receiver.
        val intent = context.registerReceiver(null, IntentFilter(Intent.ACTION_BATTERY_CHANGED))
            ?: throw FlutterError("battery_unavailable", "No battery status broadcast", null)
        val level = intent.getIntExtra(BatteryManager.EXTRA_LEVEL, -1)
        val scale = intent.getIntExtra(BatteryManager.EXTRA_SCALE, -1)
        val percent = if (level >= 0 && scale > 0) (level * 100L / scale) else null
        val state = when (intent.getIntExtra(BatteryManager.EXTRA_STATUS, -1)) {
            BatteryManager.BATTERY_STATUS_CHARGING -> BatteryStateMessage.CHARGING
            BatteryManager.BATTERY_STATUS_FULL -> BatteryStateMessage.FULL
            BatteryManager.BATTERY_STATUS_DISCHARGING,
            BatteryManager.BATTERY_STATUS_NOT_CHARGING -> BatteryStateMessage.UNPLUGGED
            else -> BatteryStateMessage.UNKNOWN
        }
        return BatterySnapshotMessage(percent, state, powerManager.isPowerSaveMode)
    }
}

class BatteryStreamHandler(
    private val context: Context,
    private val bridge: BatteryBridge,
) : WatchBatteryStreamHandler() {
    private var receiver: BroadcastReceiver? = null

    override fun onListen(p0: Any?, sink: PigeonEventSink<BatterySnapshotMessage>) {
        onCancel(null) // guard against a double listen
        val r = object : BroadcastReceiver() {
            override fun onReceive(c: Context, i: Intent) {
                runCatching { bridge.snapshot() }
                    .onSuccess(sink::success)
                    .onFailure { sink.error("battery_unavailable", it.message, null) }
            }
        }
        val filter = IntentFilter().apply {
            addAction(Intent.ACTION_BATTERY_CHANGED)          // sticky → immediate first event
            addAction(PowerManager.ACTION_POWER_SAVE_MODE_CHANGED)
        }
        ContextCompat.registerReceiver(context, r, filter, ContextCompat.RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED)
        receiver = r
    }

    override fun onCancel(p0: Any?) {
        receiver?.let { context.unregisterReceiver(it) }
        receiver = null
    }
}
```

Register it per engine. This works with multiple engines and with reattachment after an activity is recreated:

```kotlin
// MainActivity.kt
class MainActivity : FlutterActivity() {
    private var stream: BatteryStreamHandler? = null

    override fun configureFlutterEngine(flutterEngine: FlutterEngine) {
        super.configureFlutterEngine(flutterEngine)
        val ctx = applicationContext
        val messenger = flutterEngine.dartExecutor.binaryMessenger
        val bridge = BatteryBridge(ctx)
        BatteryHostApi.setUp(messenger, bridge)
        stream = BatteryStreamHandler(ctx, bridge).also { WatchBatteryStreamHandler.register(messenger, it) }
    }

    override fun cleanUpFlutterEngine(flutterEngine: FlutterEngine) {
        BatteryHostApi.setUp(flutterEngine.dartExecutor.binaryMessenger, null)
        stream?.onCancel(null)
        stream = null
        super.cleanUpFlutterEngine(flutterEngine)
    }
}
```

No permissions are required.

## 4. iOS (Swift)

```swift
// ios/Runner/Battery/BatteryBridge.swift
import Flutter
import UIKit

final class BatteryBridge: BatteryHostApi {
  func getSnapshot() throws -> BatterySnapshotMessage { Self.currentSnapshot() }
  func isPowerSaveModeEnabled() throws -> Bool { ProcessInfo.processInfo.isLowPowerModeEnabled }

  static func currentSnapshot() -> BatterySnapshotMessage {
    let device = UIDevice.current
    let wasEnabled = device.isBatteryMonitoringEnabled
    device.isBatteryMonitoringEnabled = true
    defer { if !wasEnabled { device.isBatteryMonitoringEnabled = false } }

    let raw = device.batteryLevel // -1.0 when unknown (simulator)
    let level: Int64? = raw < 0 ? nil : Int64((raw * 100).rounded())
    let state: BatteryStateMessage
    switch device.batteryState {
    case .charging: state = .charging
    case .full: state = .full
    case .unplugged: state = .unplugged
    default: state = .unknown
    }
    return BatterySnapshotMessage(level: level, state: state,
                                  powerSaveMode: ProcessInfo.processInfo.isLowPowerModeEnabled)
  }
}

final class BatteryStreamHandler: WatchBatteryStreamHandler {
  private var observers: [NSObjectProtocol] = []

  override func onListen(withArguments arguments: Any?, sink: PigeonEventSink<BatterySnapshotMessage>) {
    onCancel(withArguments: nil)
    UIDevice.current.isBatteryMonitoringEnabled = true
    let center = NotificationCenter.default
    let names: [Notification.Name] = [
      UIDevice.batteryLevelDidChangeNotification,
      UIDevice.batteryStateDidChangeNotification,
      .NSProcessInfoPowerStateDidChange, // posted off-main; queue: .main fixes that
    ]
    observers = names.map {
      center.addObserver(forName: $0, object: nil, queue: .main) { _ in
        sink.success(BatteryBridge.currentSnapshot())
      }
    }
    sink.success(BatteryBridge.currentSnapshot()) // initial value
  }

  override func onCancel(withArguments arguments: Any?) {
    observers.forEach(NotificationCenter.default.removeObserver)
    observers.removeAll()
    UIDevice.current.isBatteryMonitoringEnabled = false
  }
}
```

```swift
// AppDelegate.swift (inside application(_:didFinishLaunchingWithOptions:), after GeneratedPluginRegistrant)
if let registrar = self.registrar(forPlugin: "BatteryBridge") {
  let messenger = registrar.messenger()
  BatteryHostApiSetup.setUp(binaryMessenger: messenger, api: BatteryBridge())
  WatchBatteryStreamHandler.register(with: messenger, streamHandler: BatteryStreamHandler())
}
```

If your app uses the newer scene/implicit-engine `AppDelegate` template, put the same registration in the engine-initialized callback instead.

The generated handler and setup names (`WatchBatteryStreamHandler`, `BatteryHostApiSetup`, `watchBattery()`) come from Pigeon's naming scheme. Confirm them against your generated files.

## 5. Verification

**Dart unit tests.** Inject a fake `BatteryHostApi` and a `StreamController<BatterySnapshotMessage>` into `PlatformBatteryMonitor`, then assert that:
- enums map to the right domain values
- an out-of-range level throws `invalid_level`
- a `PlatformException` becomes `BatteryException`
- `distinct()` suppresses duplicate snapshots
- `UnsupportedBatteryMonitor` fails with `unsupported_platform`

**Android, on a real device or emulator:**
- Toggle charging with `adb shell dumpsys battery set ac 1|0` and set the level with `adb shell dumpsys battery set level 42`. Run `adb shell dumpsys battery reset` afterward.
- Toggle Battery Saver.
- Rotate the device or recreate the activity mid-stream and confirm events resume without a leaked receiver.

**iOS, on a physical device.** The simulator reports a `nil` level and `unknown` state, so it only exercises that path.
- Plug and unplug the device.
- Toggle Low Power Mode.
- Background and foreground the app while subscribed.
- Cancel the subscription and confirm the observers are removed.

**Both platforms:**
- Cancelling and re-listening produces exactly one active native listener.
- A hot restart doesn't duplicate events.
